Chapter: GopPrograms
  cites UI chapter Widget
  cites UI chapter Layout
  cites UI chapter Theme

 The launcher. The sidebar was a flat column of one button per app, so
 every app cost a row of the only column there is, and at 1024x768 it was
 full at thirteen. Groups here rather than rows there is what makes the
 fourteenth app free.

 An entry answers the SCANCODE its key would have produced. `desk-loop`
 already turns a scancode into a pane, so the launcher adds no second
 dispatch table -- the one nobody presses is the one that rots, which is
 the reason `GopCalc` does the same with its keypad. Adding an app here
 means adding it to `gpr-entries` and nothing else.

 We say:

Section: Geometry

  gpr-sel-cell : Integer = 0

Section: The Programs

 Each entry is a group name, a label, and the scancode the desk already
 answers for that pane. A row whose group repeats the row above it is
 drawn without a heading, so the group column is the sort order and the
 heading at once.

  GprEntry = record {
    ge-group : Text,
    ge-label : Text,
    ge-scan : Integer,
    ge-icon : Text
  }

  gpr-entries : List GprEntry = [
    GprEntry { ge-group = "Accessories", ge-label = "Clock", ge-scan = 37, ge-icon = "clock" },
    GprEntry { ge-group = "Accessories", ge-label = "Calculator", ge-scan = 46, ge-icon = "grid" },
    GprEntry { ge-group = "Accessories", ge-label = "Calendar", ge-scan = 38, ge-icon = "calendar" },
    GprEntry { ge-group = "Accessories", ge-label = "Aquarium", ge-scan = 16, ge-icon = "eye" },
    GprEntry { ge-group = "Accessories", ge-label = "Sheets", ge-scan = 31, ge-icon = "grid" },
    GprEntry { ge-group = "Productivity", ge-label = "Text Editor", ge-scan = 18, ge-icon = "edit" },
    GprEntry { ge-group = "Productivity", ge-label = "Issues", ge-scan = 23, ge-icon = "bell" },
    GprEntry { ge-group = "Productivity", ge-label = "Files", ge-scan = 33, ge-icon = "folder" },
    GprEntry { ge-group = "Productivity", ge-label = "Console", ge-scan = 20, ge-icon = "terminal" },
    GprEntry { ge-group = "Productivity", ge-label = "Browser", ge-scan = 48, ge-icon = "cloud" },
    GprEntry { ge-group = "Productivity", ge-label = "Review", ge-scan = 19, ge-icon = "check" },
    GprEntry { ge-group = "Graphics", ge-label = "3D View", ge-scan = 4, ge-icon = "image" },
    GprEntry { ge-group = "Graphics", ge-label = "Diffusion", ge-scan = 32, ge-icon = "star" },
    GprEntry { ge-group = "Settings", ge-label = "Appearance", ge-scan = 30, ge-icon = "settings" },
    GprEntry { ge-group = "Settings", ge-label = "System Info", ge-scan = 50, ge-icon = "info" },
    GprEntry { ge-group = "Settings", ge-label = "Web Server", ge-scan = 17, ge-icon = "cloud" },
    GprEntry { ge-group = "Settings", ge-label = "Program Runner", ge-scan = 25, ge-icon = "code" }
  ]

  gpr-count : Integer
  gpr-count = list-length gpr-entries

  gpr-at : Integer -> GprEntry
  gpr-at (i) = list-at gpr-entries i

Section: State

  gpr-sel : Integer -> Integer
  gpr-sel (ps) = peek-32 ps gpr-sel-cell

  gpr-set-sel : Integer, Integer -> Integer
  gpr-set-sel (ps) (i) =
    poke-32 ps gpr-sel-cell (if i < 0 then 0 else if i >= gpr-count then gpr-count - 1 else i)

  gpr-ensure : Integer -> Integer
  gpr-ensure (ps) = gpr-set-sel ps (gpr-sel ps)

Section: The Tree

 A heading is drawn only when the group changes, so the first row of each
 group carries it and the rest do not.

  gpr-heads : Integer -> Boolean
  gpr-heads (i) =
    if i <= 0 then True
    else (gpr-at i).ge-group /= (gpr-at (i - 1)).ge-group

  gpr-mark : Integer, Integer -> Text
  gpr-mark (i) (sel) = if i == sel then "> " else "  "

 The row's icon is painted after the layout by `desk-gpr-icons`, which has
 the laid rectangle this does not. What the row owes it is the SPACE: the
 icon lands at `gpr-mark-cells` and is `gpr-icon-cells` wide, and the
 gutter is one cell WIDER than the icon so the label does not sit flush
 against it. Measured on the glass 2026-08-20 with a two-cell gutter: every
 label touched its icon. These numbers live here rather than in the desk
 because the label they carve up is written here.

  gpr-mark-cells : Integer = 2

  gpr-icon-cells : Integer = 2

  gpr-gutter : Text = "   "

  gpr-key-name : Integer -> Text
  gpr-key-name (sc) =
    if sc == 20 then "t"
    else if sc == 18 then "e"
    else if sc == 33 then "f"
    else if sc == 4 then "3"
    else if sc == 16 then "q"
    else if sc == 32 then "d"
    else if sc == 48 then "b"
    else if sc == 23 then "i"
    else if sc == 19 then "r"
    else if sc == 38 then "l"
    else if sc == 46 then "c"
    else if sc == 50 then "m"
    else if sc == 30 then "a"
    else if sc == 37 then "k"
    else "?"

 The KEY IS NO LONGER IN THE LABEL. It read `Files   (f)`, and there is no
 longer an `f` to press: Damian removed the app-launch hotkeys on 2026-08-26.
 `ge-scan` stays, because it is also the id a menu row ANSWERS with and the
 desk's open path is keyed on it; what went is the promise to the reader.

  gpr-row : Integer, Integer -> WidgetNode
  gpr-row (i) (sel) =
    let e = gpr-at i
    in let btn = widget-button ("gpr-" & e.ge-label) (gpr-mark i sel & gpr-gutter & e.ge-label)
    in widget-set-min-h btn 22

 Two columns, not one. Nothing clips a widget panel to its box, so a
 single column of sixteen rows drew straight through the help line and
 the taskbar below it -- measured on the glass, not feared. Splitting the
 groups across columns is also what a program manager looks like, so the
 shape that fits is the shape that was wanted.

 `gpr-split` is an ENTRY index and must land on a group boundary, or the
 group whose rows it cuts loses its heading in the second column.

  gpr-split : Integer = 11

  gpr-rows : Integer, Integer, Integer, List WidgetNode -> List WidgetNode
  gpr-rows (i) (stop) (sel) (acc) =
    if i >= stop then acc
    else let acc1 = if gpr-heads i then list-push acc (widget-set-min (widget-label ("gpr-h-" & (gpr-at i).ge-group) ((gpr-at i).ge-group)) 0 20) else acc
    in gpr-rows (i + 1) stop sel (list-push acc1 (gpr-row i sel))

  gpr-tree : Integer -> WidgetNode
  gpr-tree (ps) =
    let sel = gpr-sel ps
    in let left = widget-panel "gpr-left" DirColumn 2 (gpr-rows 0 gpr-split sel [])
    in let right = widget-panel "gpr-right" DirColumn 2 (gpr-rows gpr-split gpr-count sel [])
    in let cols = widget-panel "gpr-cols" DirRow 8 [widget-set-flex left 1, widget-set-flex right 1]
    in let help = widget-set-min (widget-label "gpr-help" "up/down choose   Enter opens") 0 20
    in widget-panel "gpr-pane" DirColumn 6 [widget-set-flex cols 1, help]

Section: The Menu Tree

 THE SAME MODEL, COLLAPSED. The window above shows every entry at once
 because it is given the whole content region; the start menu is a box above
 the taskbar pill and has about 400 logical pixels of room at 1600x900, which
 the flat list does not fit -- measured on the glass, where the Shutdown row
 landed past the bottom of the screen and the taskbar disappeared behind it.

 So the menu shows the GROUPS, and the group holding the selection is the one
 that shows its apps. That is not a second model: the rows are `gpr-row`, the
 ids are the same ids, `gpr-id-scan` resolves them the same way, and the open
 group is a FUNCTION of `gpr-sel` rather than state of its own -- so a row
 chosen in either presentation is the row chosen in both, and up and down
 walking off the end of a group opens the next one with no code to do it.

 A heading is a button here where it is a label in the window, because here
 it can be clicked. Its id is prefixed so `gpr-id-scan` cannot match it: a
 heading must not answer a scancode, and the two namespaces sharing a prefix
 is how it would.

  gpr-group-prefix : Text = "gprg-"

  gpr-open-group : Integer -> Text
  gpr-open-group (ps) = (gpr-at (gpr-sel ps)).ge-group

 The index of the group's FIRST entry, offset by one so that zero can mean
 "not a heading". The caller sets the selection to it, which opens the group.

  gpr-group-scan : Text, Integer -> Integer
  gpr-group-scan (id) (i) =
    if i >= gpr-count then 0
    else if gpr-heads i & id == (gpr-group-prefix & (gpr-at i).ge-group) then i + 1
    else gpr-group-scan id (i + 1)

  gpr-group-mark : Boolean -> Text
  gpr-group-mark (open) = if open then "- " else "+ "

 Six open rows is what the tightest glass fits: at 1600x900 the chrome lays
 out in 800x450, where six rows measured a band at 406..442 and a seventh
 pushed it to 454, off the glass. A larger group shows six at a time, the
 window follows the selection, and the group's heading says which six.

  gpr-open-max : Integer = 6

  GprWindow = record {
    gw-lo : Integer,
    gw-hi : Integer,
    gw-note : Text
  }

 The table is bound once and scanned through the binding: every mention of
 `gpr-entries`, and so every `gpr-at`, rebuilds all seventeen entries, 696
 bytes (measured 2026-09-23), and a scan by `gpr-at` cost 19,512 a menu build.

  gpr-group-first : List GprEntry, Text, Integer -> Integer
  gpr-group-first (es) (grp) (i) =
    if i >= list-length es then i
    else if (list-at es i).ge-group == grp then i
    else gpr-group-first es grp (i + 1)

  gpr-group-end : List GprEntry, Text, Integer -> Integer
  gpr-group-end (es) (grp) (i) =
    if i >= list-length es then i
    else if (list-at es i).ge-group == grp then gpr-group-end es grp (i + 1)
    else i

  gpr-window : Integer, Integer -> GprWindow
  gpr-window (ps) (cap) =
    let es = gpr-entries
    in let sel = gpr-sel ps
    in let grp = (list-at es sel).ge-group
    in let g0 = gpr-group-first es grp 0
    in let n = gpr-group-end es grp g0 - g0
    in let raw = sel - g0 - cap + 1
    in let first = if n <= cap then 0 else if raw < 0 then 0 else if raw > n - cap then n - cap else raw
    in let shown = if n < cap then n else cap
    in let note = if n <= cap then "" else "  " & integer-to-text (first + 1) & "-" & integer-to-text (first + shown) & " of " & integer-to-text n
    in GprWindow { gw-lo = g0 + first, gw-hi = g0 + first + shown, gw-note = note }

  gpr-group-row : Integer, Boolean, Text -> WidgetNode
  gpr-group-row (i) (open) (note) =
    let e = gpr-at i
    in let btn = widget-button (gpr-group-prefix & e.ge-group) (gpr-group-mark open & e.ge-group & note)
    in widget-set-min-h btn 22

  gpr-menu-rows : Integer, Integer, Text, GprWindow, List WidgetNode -> List WidgetNode
  gpr-menu-rows (i) (sel) (open) (win) (acc) =
    if i >= gpr-count then acc
    else let e = gpr-at i
    in let here = e.ge-group == open
    in let acc1 = if gpr-heads i then list-push acc (gpr-group-row i here (if here then win.gw-note else "")) else acc
    in let acc2 = if here & i >= win.gw-lo & i < win.gw-hi then list-push acc1 (gpr-row i sel) else acc1
    in gpr-menu-rows (i + 1) sel open win acc2

 ACROSS, THE GROUPS ARE COLUMNS AND THE COLUMNS ARE THE ROW. Damian,
 2026-09-07, for a band docked left or right: "in left and right mode should
 layout the cobblestone menu horizontally instead of vertically". Flowing the
 rows above straight into a `DirRow` is the wrong reading of that: the open
 group's entries would sit inline BETWEEN the next two headings, so the list
 would say which group it belongs to only by position. A heading with its own
 entries beneath it says it structurally, and a closed group is then a column
 of one.

 The column ids take their own prefix. `gpr-id-scan` and `gpr-group-scan` both
 match on a prefix, so a third kind of node sharing either one would answer a
 click meant for a row or a heading; this is the same collision the heading
 prefix exists to avoid, one level up.

  gpr-col-prefix : Text = "gprc-"

  gpr-group-items : Integer, GprWindow, Integer, List WidgetNode -> List WidgetNode
  gpr-group-items (sel) (win) (i) (acc) =
    if i >= win.gw-hi then acc
    else if i < win.gw-lo then gpr-group-items sel win (i + 1) acc
    else gpr-group-items sel win (i + 1) (list-push acc (gpr-row i sel))

  gpr-menu-cols : Integer, Integer, Text, GprWindow, List WidgetNode -> List WidgetNode
  gpr-menu-cols (i) (sel) (open) (win) (acc) =
    if i >= gpr-count then acc
    else if gpr-heads i == False then gpr-menu-cols (i + 1) sel open win acc
    else
      let g = (gpr-at i).ge-group
      in let head = list-push [] (gpr-group-row i (g == open) (if g == open then win.gw-note else ""))
      in let kids = if g == open then gpr-group-items sel win 0 head else head
      in gpr-menu-cols (i + 1) sel open win (list-push acc (widget-panel (gpr-col-prefix & g) DirColumn 2 kids))

  gpr-menu-tree-cap : Integer, Boolean, Integer -> WidgetNode
  gpr-menu-tree-cap (ps) (across) (cap) =
    let win = gpr-window ps cap
    in if across then widget-panel "gpr-menu" DirRow 8 (gpr-menu-cols 0 (gpr-sel ps) (gpr-open-group ps) win [])
    else widget-panel "gpr-menu" DirColumn 2 (gpr-menu-rows 0 (gpr-sel ps) (gpr-open-group ps) win [])

  gpr-menu-tree : Integer, Boolean -> WidgetNode
  gpr-menu-tree (ps) (across) = gpr-menu-tree-cap ps across gpr-open-max

Section: Keys

 The answer is a scancode the desk already dispatches, or 0 for "nothing
 happened". Escape is the caller's business and is not handled here.

  gpr-key : Integer, Integer -> Integer
  gpr-key (ps) (sc) =
    if sc == 72 then let d = gpr-set-sel ps (gpr-sel ps - 1) in 0
    else if sc == 80 then let d = gpr-set-sel ps (gpr-sel ps + 1) in 0
    else if sc == 28 then (gpr-at (gpr-sel ps)).ge-scan
    else 0

 A clicked row answers the same scancode its Enter would have, and the id
 carries the label because that is what the widget was built with.

  gpr-id-scan : Text, Integer -> Integer
  gpr-id-scan (id) (i) =
    if i >= gpr-count then 0
    else if id == ("gpr-" & (gpr-at i).ge-label) then (gpr-at i).ge-scan
    else gpr-id-scan id (i + 1)

